## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical iOS fundamentals: Codable data models, file-based persistence with atomic writes, and SwiftUI data flow. It also exercises UX concerns like debounce for responsiveness and accessibility considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Codable data model for Note with title, body, tags, and date
- File I/O in Documents with atomic write pattern
- SwiftUI state management using @Published and binding for filters
- Combine debouncing for search input
- Tag-based filtering logic and accessibility labeling

## Code Example
```javascript
import Foundation

struct Note: Codable, Identifiable {
  var id: UUID = UUID()
  var title: String
  var body: String
  var tags: [String]
  var date: Date = Date()
}

final class NotesStore: ObservableObject {
  @Published var notes: [Note] = []
  private let fileURL: URL = FileManager.default
    .urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
    .appendingPathComponent("notes.json")

  init() { load() }

  func load() {
    guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: fileURL) else { return }
    if let loaded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data) {
      notes = loaded
    }
  }

  func save() {
    let tempURL = fileURL.appendingPathExtension("tmp")
    guard let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(notes) else { return }
    try? data.write(to: tempURL)
    try? FileManager.default.replaceItemAtURL(fileURL, withItemAtURL: tempURL)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to thousands of notes while keeping memory usage low?
- How would you test atomic write correctness and debounce behavior?
- How would you migrate to Core Data or add cloud sync later?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a robust, real-time data layer that stays responsive under network variability, using concurrency primitives and local caching.

## Key Concepts

- Swift concurrency with async/await and TaskGroups
- Local cache using SQLite
- Delta-based merge with per-item versioning
- Conflict resolution and ordering guarantees
- Test strategy for latency, reordering, and memory pressure

## Code Example

```swift
struct Post { let id: String; var version: Int; var content: String }
struct Delta { let id: String; let version: Int; let content: String }

func merge(_ deltas: [Delta], into cache: inout [String: Post]) -> [Post] {
  for d in deltas {
    if var existing = cache[d.id] {
      if d.version > existing.version {
        existing.version = d.version
        existing.content = d.content
        cache[d.id] = existing
      }
    } else {
      cache[d.id] = Post(id: d.id, version: d.version, content: d.content)
    }
  }
  return Array(cache.values)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and mitigate race conditions in the delta merge?
- How would you extend to multi-device sync and server-driven conflict resolution?
